{"candidates":[{"analogy":{"adopted_commitment_refs":[],"disanalogies":["Ensemble members are predictive models evaluated against a fixed validation set; rival positions are argumentative claims with shifting warrant semantics.","Ensemble pruning is typically performance-driven (accuracy/loss); criticism retirement is warrant-threshold-driven (argumentative coverage).","Ensembles usually have a stable validation set; DeepReason's criticism targets shift as new rivals are accepted, changing the criticism landscape dynamically."],"overturn_conditions":["If published ensemble-methods literature shows that diversity without pruning can still converge to a stable subset, the analogy weakens and the retirement failure may stem from warrant semantics rather than budget scaling.","If DeepReason's criticism mechanism already includes per-target budget scaling not visible in the evidence blocks, the coverage-debt diagnosis is wrong."],"shared_structure":["Ensemble methods add diverse models without pruning; DeepReason's schools add rival positions without retiring them.","In both cases, diversity grows but individual members are never stress-tested to elimination.","Coverage debt in ensembles (untested model combinations) maps to criticism coverage debt (untested rival positions accumulating warrants below threshold)."],"source_memory_refs":["SRC_001"],"transfer_claims":["Just as ensemble pruning thresholds prevent unbounded diversity growth, a criticism retirement threshold indexed to rivalry size could stabilize the rival population.","Dynamic budget allocation (more criticism per target when N is small) mirrors adaptive ensemble pruning strategies that reallocate resources to under-tested members.","Coverage-debt metrics from ensemble literature (e.g., fraction of member-pairs never jointly tested) could directly measure DeepReason's criticism gap."]},"claim":"Batched criticism distributes a fixed attention budget across all accepted rivals, so per-target criticism density drops as rivalry grows (18→60). If retirement requires a threshold number of per-target warrants and the budget does not scale with rivalry size, coverage debt grows super-linearly and no rival ever reaches the retirement threshold — explaining the observed zero retirements.","counterconditions":["If the criticism budget B scales linearly with N(t), per-target density stays constant and retirements should occur — falsifying the super-linear debt prediction.","If the retirement threshold τ is very low (1–2 warrants), even diluted criticism should retire some positions within a few batches — contradicting the zero-retirement observation.","If criticism is targeted (concentrated on high-priority rivals) rather than uniformly distributed across all N, some targets could still reach τ regardless of N growth.","If rivals are not monotonically added but also naturally decay or self-retire without formal criticism, N(t) could plateau and the model's monotonic-growth assumption breaks.","If the evidence blocks show that criticism budget already scales with rivalry size, the coverage-debt diagnosis is wrong and the problem lies elsewhere (e.g., warrant quality or threshold calibration)."],"evidence_refs":[],"mechanism":"Let N(t) = number of accepted rivals at batch t, B = fixed criticism budget per batch, τ = retirement threshold (warrants per target). Per-target criticism per batch = B/N(t). Accumulated warrants on target i after T batches ≤ B·T/N(t). Since N(t) grows monotonically (rivals added but not retired), B·T/N(t) decreases relative to any fixed τ once N(t) > B·T/τ. With N growing from 18 to 60 and B held constant, per-target density falls by ~3.3×, pushing all targets below τ. The criticism loop thus acts like an ensemble that adds diverse members without ever pruning: diversity accumulates but no individual member is stress-tested to failure. A sandboxed Python simulation (simulation_mode sandboxed_python_v1) could model N(t) growth under fixed vs. scaled B, plotting per-target accumulated warrants vs. τ to discriminate between the super-linear debt prediction (zero retirements under fixed B) and the linear-density prediction (retirements occur when B scales with N). Directed research fetch: https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ensemble_learning for published pruning-vs-diversity tradeoffs that bear on the design.","optional_refs":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","e6e6345c01447a7cb9547070c63ca29abb8f9c547a6f2d56fc2681eae97de249","db03ce852ae608b96a7e21bc1f75c0bb73c27c7c51acbdcd1879c214233a4043"],"sidecar":{"requested_context_aliases":["74fde5320d6c79520ea82d22a3e0c25a5ecc8e0cb50c3197058c5a3b0ecd2845","8e1eb4f6e21b65191f84fa3b5d2818d434ad498060eb9e4b99331a6e86cf26d6","e6e6345c01447a7cb9547070c63ca29abb8f9c547a6f2d56fc2681eae97de249","db03ce852ae608b96a7e21bc1f75c0bb73c27c7c51acbdcd1879c214233a4043"],"search_signal":"productive"},"typicality":0.78}],"simulation_proposals":[]}